WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.462
This list has ten steps. This one
has seven. Both bake the same cake.

2
00:00:09.682 --> 00:00:14.605
The seven-step one is concise. It
does the whole job with no waste.

3
00:00:15.105 --> 00:00:18.680
Efficient means doing the
whole job with no waste.

4
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.405
Open the door. Open the door.
Walk in. The door is already open.

5
00:00:23.625 --> 00:00:27.155
Step two is waste. Cut
it, and you still walk in.

6
00:00:27.375 --> 00:00:32.901
Get a cup. Wave at the cat. Pour the
juice. The wave does nothing for the juice.

7
00:00:33.121 --> 00:00:37.718
Cut the wave, and the juice still
gets poured. That is concise.

8
00:00:38.218 --> 00:00:42.073
Here is an algorithm for baking
a cake. It has ten steps.

9
00:00:42.293 --> 00:00:45.730
Phone a friend is a nice call,
but it does nothing for the cake.

10
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:51.337
Mix it all together. Mix it again,
the same way. It is already mixed.

11
00:00:51.557 --> 00:00:56.247
The second mix is waste. So is
the funny hat. Cut all three.

12
00:00:56.467 --> 00:01:00.693
Ten steps became seven, and every
one of the seven does something.

13
00:01:00.913 --> 00:01:05.464
Ask it of every step: cut this,
and does the cake still get baked?

14
00:01:05.964 --> 00:01:10.144
Now wrap a present for Nora.
Nine steps, with waste in them.

15
00:01:10.364 --> 00:01:15.472
Wrap the box in paper. Unwrap it
to check the teddy. Wrap it again.

16
00:01:15.692 --> 00:01:20.568
Those two steps undo each other.
Cut both, and the paper stays on.

17
00:01:20.788 --> 00:01:25.339
Counting to one hundred does nothing
for the present. Cut that too.

18
00:01:25.559 --> 00:01:30.946
Nine steps became six: box,
lid, paper, tape, ribbon, tag.

19
00:01:31.446 --> 00:01:36.833
Water three plants. Fill the can,
pour on a plant, walk to the next one.

20
00:01:37.053 --> 00:01:41.976
That run of three steps comes round again
for the second plant, and for the third.

21
00:01:42.196 --> 00:01:45.540
Written out long, that is
eleven steps on the page.

22
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.754
Instead, write the run once
and say repeat three times.

23
00:01:49.974 --> 00:01:54.339
Eleven steps become five, and the
three plants still get watered.

24
00:01:54.559 --> 00:02:00.318
Laying the table for four is the same.
Plate, fork, knife, cup, four times over.

25
00:02:00.538 --> 00:02:03.370
Eighteen steps become six with a repeat.

26
00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:08.189
A concise algorithm is easier to
follow, because there is less to read.

27
00:02:08.409 --> 00:02:11.660
It is easier to understand,
because every step matters.

28
00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.942
And it is easier to edit. Change the
repeated run once, and all three change.

29
00:02:17.162 --> 00:02:20.784
Computers like it too.
Fewer steps is less work.

30
00:02:21.284 --> 00:02:26.718
An efficient algorithm is concise:
only the steps the task needs.

31
00:02:26.938 --> 00:02:30.607
Waste is a step done twice, or
one that does nothing at all.

32
00:02:30.827 --> 00:02:34.356
Steps that come round again
are written once with a repeat.
